87:    - The seed pixel is only a lookup key; the real content comes from decoding the palette cell and repeating that decoded pattern across the whole enclosed region.\n188:    \"\"\"\n189:    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n190:    assert g.ndim == 2 and g.size > 0\n191:\n192:    bg = _mode(g)\n193:    grey = _find_structure_color(g, bg)\n194:    orientation, split_idx, mapping, palette_mask = _palette_info(g, grey, bg)\n195:\n196:    out = g.copy()\n197:    out[palette_mask] = bg\n198:\n199:    if orientation == \"bottom\":\n200:        play = g[:split_idx, :]\n201:        row_offset = 0\n202:        col_offset = 0\n203:    else:\n204:        play = g[:, :split_idx]\n205:        row_offset = 0\n\n445:def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n446:    for _ in range(MAX_GEN_ATTEMPTS):\n447:        grid = _gen_attempt(_RNG)\n448:        if grid is not None:\n449:            return grid\n450:    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate a valid palette-fill puzzle\")\n\n=== /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed581286445/11/2.py\n188:def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n189:    \"\"\"Group same-color fragments into rectangles, then stack the rectangles concentrically by area.\n190:\n191:    Input structure:\n192:    - The background is black.\n193:    - Several colors appear as separate monochrome 4-connected fragments scattered across the grid.\n194:    - All fragments of the same color can be translated, without rotation or reflection, to tile one solid rectangle.\n195:    - Different colors correspond to different rectangle areas.\n196:\n197:    Rule:\n198:    - Group the fragments by color and ignore their original absolute positions.\n199:    - For each color, find the unique rectangle dimensions whose filled area can be tiled exactly by translated copies of that color's fragments.\n200:    - Sort the reconstructed rectangles by area and draw them centered on a new canvas, with the largest rectangle first and progressively smaller rectangles painted on top